---
// firma2-measure-definition — the METADATA BAND of a performance measure's
// setup page: the set-once identity facts (name, program, definition, reporter
// guidance), collapsed to one line and opened when you want to change them.
//
// WHAT LEFT, AND WHY. This band used to also hold quantity, unit, decimal
// places and the counting rule. Those are not metadata — they are the record's
// core, the thing the page exists to build — and filing the counting rule next
// to a precision picker was the jumble the four-zone restructure removed. They
// live in firma2-measure-record now ("What each entry records"), beside the
// primary category they qualify. What stays here is everything you set once at
// creation and rarely touch again: identity, filing, and the two prose fields.
//
// WHY THIS REPLACED THE CONFIG RAIL, which was itself a considered design and is
// worth burying properly rather than deleting.
//
// The rail was right about one thing and it still holds: a column of empty
// inputs makes the DEFINITION the hero, and an input renders the same whether it
// holds an answer or nothing, so a measure eight-tenths finished looked exactly
// like one not started. The rail fixed that by collapsing every answered setting
// to a `label · value` line — you read the record, not the form.
//
// What it got wrong was WHICH content deserved a 22rem column: everything, at
// one density, with the genuinely deliberative parts in the narrowest space on
// the page. The four-zone restructure finished that separation — the record's
// core and both question lists took the wide column; the set-once facts came
// here, into a band that states them in one line and opens to a form laid out
// at full width. Nine collapsible rows became one collapsible band, which is
// the same idea applied once instead of nine times.
//
// THE SUMMARY IS THE MEASURE'S FILING LINE: `Output measure · Forest Health &
// Fuels` — what kind of statement this measure makes, and where it rolls up.
// The kind part is FIXED (set at creation, restated in full inside the body);
// the program part is the one selectable identity fact, and it degrades into
// the checklist: unset, it falls back to the field's own outstandingFields()
// label ("Program") in secondary ink. The measure's NAME is deliberately not in
// the line — the page header directly above renders it live, and printing it
// twice a heading apart would be the page reading itself aloud.
//
// READINESS LEFT THE BAND. The old trailing-edge count ("3 settings needed
// before publishing") counted the WHOLE measure — including fields that now
// live two zones below — so a collapsed band wearing it would send the author
// hunting in here for a gap that lives elsewhere. The whole-measure count now
// rides beside Save in the page header, which is the action it gates; this
// band's `open` answers only for its own four fields.
//
// COMPOSED, NOT BUILT, and with no exceptions left. There used to be one — a
// native <details> — and flattening the band retired it along with the
// bcn-lego-checked note that argued for it. Every value-collecting control is a
// Lit lego (esa-text-field, esa-select, esa-textarea) registered in the client
// script and used as a custom element; the layout is `.switcher` from
// @esa/tokens; and what is left of this file's own CSS is colour and margin.
//
// EVERY LABEL IS A LOAD-BEARING STRING, not a copy choice. outstandingFields()
// in the data module reports what a measure still needs by these exact words —
// "Measure name", "Definition", "Program", "Reporter guidance". A reader told
// "Program" is missing by the header's readiness line must not have to work out
// which of these fields that is.
//
// EVERY VOCABULARY IS IMPORTED. CLASSIFICATIONS comes from the data module,
// which re-exports the PROJECTS' own list — a measure and a project must offer
// the same vocabulary or a roll-up across the two splits a bucket in half.
//
// SHADOW DOM IS WHY EVERY LABEL SITS ON ITS CONTROL. The Lit controls render
// into shadow DOM and carry their own label / help-text / required. A <label>
// outside a shadow root can never be associated with the input inside it, so
// nothing here is wrapped in esa-form-field and no wrapper supplies a control's
// name. (Hub pattern: ecology/apps/site/src/pages/patterns/form-section.astro,
// "Composition notes".)
//
// ONE CONTROL SIZE: md, everywhere, no exceptions.
//
// THE IDS ARE A CONTRACT. src/lib/measure-editor.ts reads and writes the
// controls on this page BY ID; this band holds fmc-name, fmc-classifications,
// fmc-definition and fmc-guidance (fmc-quantity, fmc-unit, fmc-decimals and
// fmc-rule moved to firma2-measure-record, ids intact). They may move in the
// DOM — they keep doing so — but renaming one silently detaches a field from
// the draft: it will still render, still accept typing, and never be saved.
import type { PerformanceMeasureDefinition } from '../../data/firma2-performance-measures';
import {
  measureDisplayName,
  measureKind,
} from '../../data/firma2-performance-measures';

interface Props {
  /** The measure being configured. Every field on it may be empty or unset. */
  measure: PerformanceMeasureDefinition;
}

const { measure } = Astro.props;

// THE KIND. Output or outcome, read from the kind's own record rather than
// compared against the string 'output', so adding a third kind upstream changes
// this component without touching it.
const kind = measureKind(measure.kind);
const kindDetail = `Records ${kind.description}. Reported by ${kind.reportedBy}.`;
const kindFixed = 'Set at creation. Changing it would invalidate every record filed.';

// NO FILING LINE, AND NO OPEN STATE. Both went with the <details>. The line
// ("Output measure · Forest Health & Fuels") existed for exactly one reason: it
// was the one-line stand-in for content that was hidden. Nothing is hidden now,
// and both of its halves are on screen a few lines below it — the kind as this
// block's own heading, the program as the Program field's value. Restating them
// above the fields they summarise would be the page reading itself aloud.
//
// That deleted the whole apparatus behind it: the unset-fallback map, its
// serialised payload, the data-fmd-part handles, and the MEASURE_CHANGE_EVENT
// listener that repainted the program part live so the summary could not drift
// from the field. A summary that cannot drift because it does not exist is the
// cheaper version of the same guarantee.
---

<!-- The region has a NAME but no VISIBLE title, for the reason the config rail
     gave and which survives the move: the page header already names the measure,
     and a band captioned "Settings" beside its own contents is a label on
     something a reader can see the whole of. The name still has to exist for a
     screen reader choosing between this band, the editor and the previews, so it
     is a visually-hidden heading rather than an aria-label — a heading also lands
     in the rotor's heading list, which is how anyone actually navigates a long
     page.

     Built from measureDisplayName() rather than `measure.name` so an unnamed
     draft announces "Settings for Untitled measure" instead of "Settings for" —
     the same fallback string every other surface of this record uses. -->
<section
  class="firma2-measure-definition-region"
  aria-labelledby="fmc-region-name"
  data-firma2-measure-definition
>
  <h2 id="fmc-region-name" class="visually-hidden">Settings for {measureDisplayName(measure)}</h2>

  <!-- NO <details>. This was a collapsible band and the collapse has gone.
       It earned its place while this sat at the top of the wide column: four
       set-once fields, rarely touched, spending the page's most valuable strip —
       so it collapsed to a line and opened when asked.
       In the rail neither half of that argument survives. The rail is a narrow
       column that scrolls, so the fields cost the page nothing by being open;
       and a disclosure is friction, which is the thing this product has decided
       it removes rather than adds (see the product brief, "always editable,
       always saved"). One click between noticing a wrong program and fixing it
       is one click too many when the click buys nothing.
       What went with it: the summary, its caret, the surface it drew, and every
       line of script that kept the summary honest. -->
  <div class="firma2-measure-definition__body stack" data-gap="lg">
      <!-- THE KIND — STATED, NOT OFFERED. The summary line above spends the
           kind's NAME as its fixed subject ("Output measure · …"); this block is
           the full statement — what the measure records, who reports it, and the
           fact that the answer is closed — which is what you need in view while
           EDITING, and the band is open precisely then.

           bcn-lego-checked: this block is three paragraphs of text, so the
           question was never "which control" but "which container", and the
           answer is none.
             · esa-select disabled — the treatment this explicitly rules out.
               Disabled chrome claims "not available TO YOU", which invites the
               author to look for the permission or the unlock. The truth is
               different and stronger: the question was asked and answered at
               creation, and re-answering it would invalidate every record already
               filed against the measure. A control that cannot be operated is a
               worse way to say "decided" than a sentence that says it.
             · esa-badge / esa-pill — checked and rejected on GRAMMAR, not looks.
               On this page a pill means a status you can read off the header;
               putting the kind in one would enlist it in that vocabulary.
             · esa-alert-box — an alert reports a problem or a change. Being an
               output is neither; it is the ordinary state of a correct measure.
             · esa-card / esa-container — a box would make the statement a
               section, equal in weight to the settings, and add a boundary where
               the point is that this is the frame the settings sit inside.
           Checked Beacon: its read-only entity facts are plain definition text
           too, and it is not cloned here in any case.
           So: no container, no fill, no border, no icon. Prominence comes from
           TYPE and fixity from the total absence of an affordance plus one
           sentence that says so. CSS below is colour and margin only. -->
      <div class="firma2-measure-definition__kind stack" data-gap="2xs">
        <!-- "{name} measure" rather than the bare vocabulary word: read cold at
             the top of a form, "Output" alone is a value with no subject. The
             noun supplies the subject and costs one word. -->
        <p class="firma2-measure-definition__kind-name typography-title-sm-strong">{kind.name} measure</p>
        <p class="firma2-measure-definition__kind-detail typography-body-md">{kindDetail}</p>
        <!-- Its own paragraph, not appended to the one above: what the measure
             records and the fact that the answer is closed are two different
             claims, and running them together buries the second. Same size, same
             ink — greying this line would restate the "unavailable" reading the
             disabled select was rejected for. -->
        <p class="firma2-measure-definition__kind-detail typography-body-md">{kindFixed}</p>
      </div>

      <!-- NAME and CLASSIFICATIONS, side by side: who this measure is and what
           it reports toward. `.switcher` puts them on one row while there is
           room and stacks them when there is not.

           CLASSIFICATIONS REPLACED PROGRAM, and the change is a set replacing a
           branch. A program is where a project FILES; a classification is what
           it is FOR — and one measure serves several at once, so forcing a
           single branch made the portfolio's real roll-up axis ("what did we
           buy toward salmon recovery") unanswerable without hand-sorting. The
           vocabulary is CLASSIFICATIONS, re-exported from the projects module
           so a measure and a project can never offer different lists.

           esa-combobox in `multiple` mode, NOT esa-select: this is a set, and
           the lego renders picked members as removable chips with a type-ahead
           filter. It is the SAME lego the project detail page opens when its own
           classifications row is edited, and the script below borrows the two
           things that make the concept look and behave the same on both screens
           — the chip skin (src/lib/combobox-chip-skin.ts) and the
           vocabulary-order commit. What it does NOT borrow is
           firma2-editable-field itself: that component is a display that becomes
           an editor, which is right for a page you read and correct, and this
           band is a form whose fields are open the whole time. Same control,
           same chip, different container — deliberately.

           Its option values ARE the labels; nothing is slugged (see
           measure-editor.ts). -->
      <div class="firma2-measure-definition__headline switcher" data-gap="md">
        <esa-text-field
          id="fmc-name"
          label="Measure name"
          required
          size="md"
          value={measure.name}
          placeholder="e.g. Acres of riparian habitat restored"></esa-text-field>

        <esa-combobox
          id="fmc-classifications"
          label="Classifications"
          required
          multiple
          size="md"
          placeholder="Search classifications"
          data-initial={JSON.stringify(measure.classifications)}
          help-text="The plan goals this measure reports toward. Pick as many as apply."></esa-combobox>
      </div>

      <!-- THE PROSE, last, as real textareas. These are the two fields on the
           record that hold authored English — the catalog's examples run two to
           three sentences each — so they are the tallest things here and they go
           at the bottom where they cost the scannable part of the form nothing.

           Side by side in a `.switcher` because they are a pair: Definition is
           what counts, Reporter guidance is what to tell the person counting, and
           an author writes them against each other. Four rows each: the median
           catalog example lands just inside that at this column width. -->
      <div class="firma2-measure-definition__prose switcher" data-gap="md">
        <esa-textarea
          id="fmc-definition"
          label="Definition"
          required
          size="md"
          rows="4"
          value={measure.definition}
          help-text="What counts toward this measure, and what does not."></esa-textarea>

        <esa-textarea
          id="fmc-guidance"
          label="Reporter guidance"
          required
          size="md"
          rows="4"
          value={measure.reporterGuidance}
          help-text="Shown beside the value box when a project reports."></esa-textarea>
      </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* NO SURFACE, AND NO PADDING. Both are deletions rather than omissions.
     The band drew esa-collapsible's card chrome — border, radius, raised fill —
     because it was a closed box sitting in the page's widest column and needed
     an edge to be a box. In the rail it is not a box: the rail is already a
     filled, bordered surface, so a card in here would be the box-inside-a-box
     the project page's rail deleted for the same reason. The padding went with
     the card for the same reason again — the rail spends its own.

     Also gone: the summary's chrome, its caret and its rotation, the sentence
     cluster's wrap rules and the unset-part ink. Every one of them styled
     something that no longer renders. */

  /* THE KIND BLOCK OWNS NO SURFACE. The record section next door is prominent
     by being ON something — its sunken band. This is prominent by TYPE alone,
     and the absence of a box is load-bearing: it is the visual difference
     between "a setting" and "a fact about this measure". */
  .firma2-measure-definition__kind-name,
  .firma2-measure-definition__kind-detail {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-content-default, #202020);
  }

  /* The layout primitive's own documented knob, set where the layout is
     declared: 40rem is where a two-up row stops being readable at md control
     size. */
  .firma2-measure-definition__headline,
  .firma2-measure-definition__prose {
    --switcher-threshold: 40rem;
  }

  /* The typography composites set type ONLY — family, size, weight, line-height —
     and nothing in tokens.css or BaseLayout resets paragraph margins. So the UA's
     `margin: 1em 0` survives and would add to the .stack's gap. Zeroing it is not
     a visual choice; it is the composite's missing half — the spine owns spacing. */
  .firma2-measure-definition__body p {
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>

<script>
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-combobox';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-text-field';
  import '@esa/ecology/esa-textarea';

  import { CLASSIFICATIONS } from '../../data/firma2-performance-measures';
  import { skinComboboxChips } from '../../lib/combobox-chip-skin';

  /**
   * THE CLASSIFICATION SET. Options and value are both PROPERTIES on the lego —
   * an array cannot survive the trip through an attribute — so both are
   * assigned after `whenDefined`, which is what guarantees the class (and its
   * accessors) exist. Assigning to a not-yet-upgraded element would create own
   * properties that shadow the accessors for the element's whole life.
   *
   * NO SLUGGING. There was a slug() here, mapping "Forest Health & Fuels" to
   * "forest-health-fuels" for the program select and back again on every read.
   * Classification option values are the labels themselves, so the round trip
   * — and the class of silent mismatch it invited — is gone. Units and counting
   * rules were never slugged either, for the reason stated in measure-editor.
   */
  customElements.whenDefined('esa-combobox').then(async () => {
    const field = document.getElementById('fmc-classifications') as
      | (HTMLElement & {
          options: { label: string; value: string }[];
          value: string[];
          updateComplete?: Promise<unknown>;
        })
      | null;
    if (!field) return;
    field.options = CLASSIFICATIONS.map((label) => ({ label, value: label }));
    try {
      const initial = JSON.parse(field.dataset.initial ?? '[]');
      if (Array.isArray(initial)) field.value = sortByVocabulary(initial as string[]);
    } catch {
      /* A malformed payload renders an empty set rather than taking the band down. */
    }

    // THE SAME CHIP THE PROJECT PAGE SHOWS. esa-combobox's own chips are
    // green-tinted, fully rounded and a type rung large; the shared recipe
    // re-skins them to this spoke's quiet chip. Without it, the one
    // classification vocabulary rendered neutral on the project detail page and
    // green here — one concept, two skins, on the only two screens that show
    // it. NO GEOMETRY ARGUMENT: firma2-editable-field passes one because its
    // row swaps between a display and an editor and nothing may move in the
    // swap; this is a plain form field with no display state to mirror, so the
    // lego's own arrangement is right.
    await Promise.resolve(field.updateComplete);
    skinComboboxChips(field);

    // COMMIT IN THE VOCABULARY'S ORDER, not click order — the same rule
    // firma2-editable-field applies when the project page commits this set. The
    // combobox appends members as they are toggled, so without this two
    // measures with identical classifications would read in different orders
    // depending on which was clicked first.
    field.addEventListener('change', () => {
      const next = sortByVocabulary(Array.isArray(field.value) ? field.value : []);
      if (next.join('\u0000') !== (field.value ?? []).join('\u0000')) field.value = next;
    });
  });

  /** The set, in CLASSIFICATIONS' own order rather than the order picked. */
  function sortByVocabulary(members: string[]): string[] {
    return (CLASSIFICATIONS as readonly string[]).filter((c) => members.includes(c));
  }

  // NO SENTENCE TO WIRE ANY MORE. There was a wireSentence() here, and it was
  // the careful kind of code: rather than listening to this band's own controls
  // — which would not have seen a draft restored from localStorage, and would
  // have given the band a second opinion about the record while two previews
  // beside it read the first — it listened to MEASURE_CHANGE_EVENT on `document`
  // and rendered the summary from the whole working record. Three panels, one
  // source, no possibility of disagreement.
  //
  // All of it went with the summary. The program is now read where it is edited,
  // in the field two lines below, so there is no second render of it to keep
  // honest. Deleting the whole mechanism is the version of that guarantee that
  // cannot regress.
</script>
